      "id": "word-buscarlo",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "buscarlo",
      "answerEn": "to look it up; to search for it",
      "noteEn": "Buscarlo combines buscar, “to look for or look up,” with lo, “it.” Lo attaches to the unchanged verb and points back to a masculine thing already mentioned.",
      "example": { "es": "Voy a buscarlo en el manual.", "en": "I am going to look it up in the manual." },
      "tags": ["word", "search", "infinitive", "attached pronoun", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-enseñe--show-subjunctive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "enseñe",
      "answerEn": "I show; I teach (special form after deja que)",
      "noteEn": "The ordinary speaker form is enseño. After deja que asks to allow the action, enseño changes to enseñe, a form used for an allowed or requested action rather than a stated fact.",
      "example": { "es": "Deja que te enseñe el mapa.", "en": "Let me show you the map." },
      "tags": ["word", "enseñar", "subjunctive", "first person", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-páginas--manual-pages",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "páginas",
      "answerEn": "pages; manual pages (documentation)",
      "noteEn": "In a computing context, páginas can refer to pages of technical documentation. A página man is a Unix-style reference page opened with the man command.",
      "example": { "es": "Consulté las páginas del manual.", "en": "I consulted the manual pages." },
      "tags": ["word", "documentation", "computing", "plural", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-coges--answer-phone",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "coges",
      "answerEn": "you pick up; you answer (the phone, informal singular)",
      "noteEn": "Coges is the present form of coger for one familiar listener. With el teléfono, it commonly means picking up or answering the phone in Spain.",
      "example": { "es": "Nunca coges el teléfono.", "en": "You never answer the phone." },
      "tags": ["word", "telephone", "coger", "present", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-debo--must",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "debo",
      "answerEn": "I must; I should",
      "noteEn": "Debo is the present form of deber used by the speaker. A following action stays in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "example": { "es": "Debo llamar a Ana.", "en": "I must call Ana." },
      "tags": ["word", "obligation", "deber", "first person", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-llego--arrive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "llego",
      "answerEn": "I arrive; I get there",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "arrival", "first person", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-hablad--vosotros-command",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "hablad",
      "answerEn": "talk; speak (command to several people, mainly in Spain)",
      "noteEn": "Hablad is the positive command for vosotros or vosotras, the informal plural “you” used mainly in Spain. It comes from hablar by replacing the final -r with -d.",
      "example": { "es": "Hablad con calma.", "en": "Speak calmly." },
      "tags": ["word", "command", "vosotros", "Spain", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-ayudar-a-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "ayudar a alguien a + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone helps another person do an action?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of ayudar with the person receiving help, then a and the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The person can be a short word such as me, te, or le: me ayuda, te ayudamos, or ayudarle. The a before the following action is required. That action stays in its dictionary form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir; only the first verb changes when needed.",
      "example": { "es": "Luis me ayuda a cocinar.", "en": "Luis helps me cook." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "help", "verb pattern", "infinitive", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-coger-el-teléfono",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "coger el teléfono",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone picks up or answers the phone?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of coger followed by el teléfono.",
      "noteEn": "Coger changes for the person and time: cojo, coges, cogió. This telephone expression is common in Spain. In regions where coger can sound vulgar, contestar el teléfono or atender el teléfono is a safer alternative.",
      "example": { "es": "¿Puedes coger el teléfono?", "en": "Can you answer the phone?" },
      "tags": ["phrase", "telephone", "Spain", "regional usage", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-linux-learning-escalation",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "de instalar Linux a compilar el kernel",
      "answerEn": "The comic compresses a Linux learner's rapid progress from a basic installation request to reading Unix manuals, switching distributions, and building a custom kernel.",
      "noteEn": "Xorg is a display system; Ubuntu, Debian, and Gentoo are Linux distributions; and the kernel is the operating system's core. Moving through those topics in a few weeks signals increasingly advanced and obsessive involvement. The final warning copies the tone of a parental public-service announcement for the joke.",
      "tags": ["concept", "Linux", "Unix", "operating systems", "xkcd joke"]
    }
  ],
